<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cagliari</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Karalis, Calaris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cagliari is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, on its southern coast. Founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> as Karalis, it was a Roman port and then the seat of the medieval Judicate of Cagliari. The Pisans built the fortified Castello quarter in 1216, and after the Aragonese conquest in the 14th century it became the capital of the Kingdom of Sardinia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cagliari.jpg" alt="Cagliari" width="320" height="569" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nilo1926 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cagliari_collage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cagliari stands on the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, at the head of a bay, on a position between the sea and a fertile plain. It is surrounded by two marshes, which gave defence against attacks from inland, and high mountains nearby offered a refuge if the settlement had to be abandoned. Its natural resources have always been its sheltered harbour, the strongly fortified hill of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-di-castro">Castel di Castro</a>, the salt of its lagoons, wheat from the Campidano plain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and other ores from the Iglesiente mines. Its mild climate allows many subtropical plants to grow.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been inhabited since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, and under the modern city lies a continuous stratification of settlement over some five thousand years. The legend told by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> writer Gaius Julius Solinus says that Caralis was founded by Aristaeus, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and the nymph Cyrene, who introduced hunting and agriculture to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. Remains of the ancient city of Karalis include the Tuvixeddu necropolis, the largest Punic necropolis still in existence, the Roman amphitheatre, traditionally called Is centu scalas, and an aqueduct.</p><p>Cagliari was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, apart from a short occupation by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> in 551 and 552 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothic-war-535-554">Gothic War</a>, and became the capital of a gradually independent Judicate, which was born around 1020 and overthrown by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Republic of Pisa</a> in 1258. Pisa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> both valued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> as a base for controlling the trade routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. The Pisan Lamberto Visconti forced the judikessa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedetta-of-cagliari">Benedetta of Cagliari</a> to give him the mount east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-igia">Santa Igia</a>, and Pisan merchants founded there the fortified city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-di-castro">Castel di Castro</a>, ancestor of modern Cagliari.</p><p>The Crown of Aragon conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> from the Pisans in the 14th century. During the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-di-castro">Castel di Castro</a>, from 1324 to 1326, the Aragonese under Alfonso IV built a stronghold on the hill of Bonaria. Cagliari was the capital of the Kingdom of Sardinia from 1324 to 1848. In 1718, after a brief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule, Cagliari and Sardinia came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>. In 1848 the Perfect Fusion merged the Savoyard possessions into one state with its parliament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, which then became the formal capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cagliari is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, an autonomous region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, with about 145,981 inhabitants. It is the seat of the University of Cagliari, founded in 1606, and of the Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Cagliari, since the 5th century. The Feast of Sant'Efisio on 1 May is its most important religious event, with a procession to Nora, where according to tradition the saint was beheaded. Its native language, official alongside Italian, is Sardinian, in the Campidanese dialect.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cagliari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>James II invaded Sardinia in 1323, taking Iglesias in 1324 and <b>Cagliari</b> in 1326, but the island resisted under the judges of Arborea for most of a century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the time Sardinia was divided between the Republic of Pisa, which held <b>Cagliari</b> and the south-west with its silver mines, Genoese noble families such as the Doria in the north, and the native Judicate of Arborea in the west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Judicate of <b>Cagliari</b>, also called Calari, took shape in southern Sardinia in the 10th and 11th centuries as Byzantine authority lapsed.</blockquote><a href="/state/judicate-of-cagliari">Judicate of Cagliari</a></li></ul></section>
